Relevant columns and why they matter  
• model_score.name / model.name : joins – they identify the data set (a “model” in the sense of the question).  
• model_score.version / model.version : joins – different experiments (versions) for the same data set.  
• model_score.step : needed to look at the different stacking stages inside one (name, version).  
• model_score.model : to separate the “Stack” model from every other (“non‑Stack”) model.  
• model_score.test_score : the metric used to decide strong / soft.  
• model.L1_model : the first‑level learner we finally have to count.  

Main conditions that will be used  
• model_score.model LIKE '%Stack%' → rows that correspond to the stacker.  
• model_score.model NOT LIKE '%Stack%' → all other level‑0 models.  

Ten exploratory / building‑block queries
```sql
-- 1. See all the different model names that appear in model_score
SELECT DISTINCT "model"
FROM "model_score"
LIMIT 20;

-- 2. Check whether and how “Stack” appears in model names
SELECT DISTINCT "model"
FROM "model_score"
WHERE "model" LIKE '%Stack%'
LIMIT 20;

-- 3. Inspect the score rows for one concrete experiment
SELECT *
FROM "model_score"
WHERE "name" = 'iris'
  AND "version" = 1
  AND "step"  = 1
LIMIT 20;

-- 4. For every (name, version, step) get the best non‑Stack score
SELECT "name",
       "version",
       "step",
       MAX("test_score") AS "max_non_stack"
FROM   "model_score"
WHERE  "model" NOT LIKE '%Stack%'
GROUP  BY "name", "version", "step"
LIMIT 20;

-- 5. Obtain the Stack score for the same triple key
SELECT "name",
       "version",
       "step",
       "test_score" AS "stack_score"
FROM   "model_score"
WHERE  "model" LIKE '%Stack%'
LIMIT 20;

-- 6. Combine #4 and #5 to label every step as strong / soft / neither
SELECT a."name",
       a."version",
       a."step",
       CASE 
            WHEN b."stack_score" >  a."max_non_stack" THEN 'strong'
            WHEN b."stack_score" =  a."max_non_stack" THEN 'soft'
       END AS "step_status"
FROM   (SELECT "name","version","step",MAX("test_score") AS "max_non_stack"
        FROM   "model_score"
        WHERE  "model" NOT LIKE '%Stack%'
        GROUP  BY "name","version","step") AS a
JOIN   (SELECT "name","version","step","test_score" AS "stack_score"
        FROM   "model_score"
        WHERE  "model" LIKE '%Stack%') AS b
ON     a."name"    = b."name"
   AND a."version" = b."version"
   AND a."step"    = b."step"
LIMIT 20;

-- 7. Escalate the step‑level label to a model‑level label (any strong → strong, else soft)
SELECT "name",
       "version",
       CASE WHEN SUM(CASE WHEN "step_status"='strong' THEN 1 END) > 0
                 THEN 'strong'
            WHEN SUM(CASE WHEN "step_status"='soft'   THEN 1 END) > 0
                 THEN 'soft'
       END AS "model_status"
FROM   ( /* query #6 here */ ) AS s
GROUP  BY "name", "version"
LIMIT 20;

-- 8. Attach the L1_model to every (name, version) and keep one row per experiment
SELECT m."L1_model",
       t."model_status"
FROM   ( /* query #7 here */ )        AS t
JOIN   (SELECT DISTINCT "name","version","L1_model"
        FROM "model") AS m
ON     t."name"    = m."name"
AND    t."version" = m."version"
LIMIT 20;

-- 9. Count how many times each L1_model occurs under each status
SELECT "L1_model",
       "model_status",
       COUNT(*) AS "n_occurrences"
FROM   ( /* query #8 here */ )
GROUP  BY "L1_model","model_status"
LIMIT 20;

--10. For every status, find the L1_model with the highest count
SELECT q."model_status",
       q."L1_model",
       q."n_occurrences"
FROM   ( /* query #9 here */ ) AS q
WHERE  q."n_occurrences" = (
        SELECT MAX(p."n_occurrences")
        FROM   ( /* query #9 here */ ) AS p
        WHERE  p."model_status" = q."model_status"
      );
```
